KG42.register("nodes-550652c5e2c97d9aec504631",{"id":"COMP-cb8f993b6ad4c7bc","code":"CLR053","label":"Cross-region allocation coupling regional balance and flow costs","definition":"Through a source-explicit regional balance relation, associate regional net exchange with cross-regional directed flows, and select the cross-regional configuration under an objective that includes interconnection flow transaction costs.","layer":"L5","template_code":"JOC","concept_id":"clearing.inter_area","concept_ids":["clearing.inter_area"],"identity_dimensions":{"definition":"Through a source-explicit regional balance relation, associate regional net exchange with cross-regional directed flows, and select the cross-regional configuration under an objective that includes interconnection flow transaction costs.","object":"Inter-regional interconnection flows and their coupled regional net exchange and generation-consumption configuration.","inputs":"Regional supply-demand or net-exchange information, interconnection representation, and flow cost rules; external exchange charges, generation costs, and interconnection permissions are provided according to the source.","operation":"Aggregate the associated regional net exchange using signed interconnection flows, include each interconnection's flow cost in configuration selection, and satisfy the quantity and coupling conditions specified by the source.","outputs":"Cross-regional flows satisfying the specified regional balance, and the related net exchange and production configuration.","boundary":"Limited to regional flow configuration in which L5/JOC costs participate; an overall cost objective or internal zero-sum alone is insufficient for inclusion. Retain the original balance signs, cost functions, and quantity domains; do not complete unsupported balance equations; do not change soft capacity into a hard upper limit."},"exclusions":[["Applies only to the three neighborhoods that adopt the geographical preference model; v=(v_12,v_13,v_23), and each interconnection retains its own geographical weight.","g retains the original signs of the import and export terms and the absolute flow charges, and is not rewritten as the differentiable transmission cost function of 27.","This projection uses only the explicitly listed N1 and N2 balances; the complete quotation's N3 equation repeats v_13, which is not corrected without authorization or used to prove whole-network conservation.","The flow domain, units, and capacity limits not explicitly given by this member remain unknown."],["Islanded interconnected microgrids must agree to cooperate to minimize total generation and transmission costs; their objective is not reduced to geographical charges.","E_{i,j} is measured in MWh and is nonnegative; A may be asymmetric, and when a_{i,j}=0 the transaction is zero; local sold quantity, purchased quantity, and residual generation retain nonnegativity conditions.","ε_i^{(s)}=e_i^T A E_i^{(s)} constrains the final feasible configuration; it is not required that each round's local solution already be consistent.","C_i and γ must be positive, monotonically increasing, convex, and twice differentiable; these requirements are not imposed on 04.","Nominal generation and transmission upper limits can be represented as soft constraints by steeply increasing costs, allowing the nominal values to be exceeded after paying additional costs.","Locally calculate purchase and sale quantities and exchange quotations, then adjust prices until supply and demand are consistent; this process is an implementation of the configuration, not a price loop shared by the two members."]],"members":[{"id":"04-energy_collectives::v141-c-R016::V::clearing.inter_area","rule_id":"v141-c-R016","market_meaning":"The inter-neighbourhood allocation model includes relative geographical transaction costs weighted by the magnitudes of energy flows on the connecting lines. Scope: Inter-neighbourhood allocations within the illustrated three-neighbourhood energy collective. Condition: When the geographical-preference formulation is adopted. Inter-neighbourhood allocations must satisfy the specified balances for N1 and N2: each neighbourhood's aggregate community exchange equals the signed sum of its connecting-line flows. Scope: The N1 and N2 balance restrictions in the illustrated collective comprising neighbourhoods N1, N2 and N3. Condition: When the three-neighbourhood geographical-preference formulation is adopted.","omega":{"market_type":"LEM_DEM","network_level":null,"jurisdiction":null,"pricing_scope":null,"time_scale":null,"participant_types":[],"trading_stage":"clearing","context_text":"Inter-neighbourhood allocations within the illustrated three-neighbourhood energy collective. | The N1 and N2 balance restrictions in the illustrated collective comprising neighbourhoods N1, N2 and N3.","unknown_fields":["network_level","jurisdiction","pricing_scope","time_scale","participant_types"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E29","v141-b-g01-E30"]},"constraint_refs":["04-energy_collectives::v141-c-R016::v141-c-R016-C001","04-energy_collectives::v141-c-R016::v141-c-R016-C002"],"decision_anchor":"clearing.inter_area","source_rule_sha256":"832badce48c31455b3a33e4e3257f117b85ec95e34b8d1b1304beda2d487009e","paper":"04-energy_collectives","rule_ref":"04-energy_collectives::v141-c-R016","node_id":"clearing.inter_area","function":"clearing.inter_area","unit_id":"04-energy_collectives::v141-a-M02::clearing.inter_area","mechanism_id":"v141-a-M02","anchor":true},{"id":"27-multiple_microgrid_trading::v141-c-R003::V::clearing.inter_area","rule_id":"v141-c-R003","market_meaning":"Computation and disclosure of a microgrid's purchase bid to potential sellers precede the inter-microgrid market-clearing process. Scope: Each iteration of distributed energy trading among interconnected islanded microgrids. Local computation of the energy quantity offered for sale precedes the distributed price-adjustment phase identified as inter-microgrid market clearing. Scope: Each iteration of distributed energy trading among interconnected islanded microgrids. Exchanged energy is allocated to minimize global operating cost while satisfying the microgrids' local demands. Scope: System-level allocation of energy exchanged among interconnected islanded microgrids. Condition: All microgrids agree to cooperate to minimize total system cost. Each microgrid's generation plus imports must equal consumption plus exports. Local subproblems constrain offered sales, purchases and residual generation to be nonnegative. Scope: Feasible inter-microgrid allocations and the local optimization subproblems used to compute them. The aggregate energy sold by each microgrid must equal the total energy bought from it by the other microgrids; this coupling constraint governs feasible allocations rather than requiring agreement at every intermediate iteration. Scope: Feasible solutions of the reformulated inter-microgrid allocation problem (4). The allocated energy transfer E_{i,j} must be zero. Scope: Directed inter-microgrid transfer allocations. Condition: There is no directed connection from μG-i to μG-j, so a_{i,j}=0. All generation and transfer cost functions must be positive valued, monotonically increasing, convex and twice differentiable. Scope: Applicability of the proposed distributed inter-microgrid cost-minimization method. Nominal generation and link-transfer bounds may be represented by steeply rising cost functions. A microgrid may exceed its nominal generation maximum at significant additional cost. Scope: Optional representation of nominal generation and transfer-capacity bounds in the inter-microgrid allocation problem. Condition: Nominal upper bounds are introduced through cost-function soft constraints.","omega":{"market_type":"DEM","network_level":null,"jurisdiction":null,"pricing_scope":null,"time_scale":null,"participant_types":[],"trading_stage":"clearing","context_text":"Applicability of the proposed distributed inter-microgrid cost-minimization method. | Directed inter-microgrid transfer allocations. | Each iteration of distributed energy trading among interconnected islanded microgrids. | Feasible inter-microgrid allocations and the local optimization subproblems used to compute them. | Feasible solutions of the reformulated inter-microgrid allocation problem (4). | Optional representation of nominal generation and transfer-capacity bounds in the inter-microgrid allocation problem. | System-level allocation of energy exchanged among interconnected islanded microgrids.","unknown_fields":["network_level","jurisdiction","pricing_scope","time_scale","participant_types"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e033","v141-b-g03-e003","v141-b-g03-e12","v141-b-g04-e004","v141-b-g06-e006","v141-b-g08-e008","v141-b-g11-e011","v141-b-g12-e012","v141-b-g15-e015","v141-b-g16-e016","v141-b-g22-e022","v141-b-g24-e024"]},"constraint_refs":["27-multiple_microgrid_trading::v141-c-R003::v141-c-R003-C001","27-multiple_microgrid_trading::v141-c-R003::v141-c-R003-C002","27-multiple_microgrid_trading::v141-c-R003::v141-c-R003-C003","27-multiple_microgrid_trading::v141-c-R003::v141-c-R003-C004","27-multiple_microgrid_trading::v141-c-R003::v141-c-R003-C005","27-multiple_microgrid_trading::v141-c-R003::v141-c-R003-C006","27-multiple_microgrid_trading::v141-c-R003::v141-c-R003-C007","27-multiple_microgrid_trading::v141-c-R003::v141-c-R003-C008"],"decision_anchor":"clearing.inter_area","source_rule_sha256":"c4fac6566f29b61465c31094a8d1014b0e576fa9ad359b563b0d945606e255a2","paper":"27-multiple_microgrid_trading","rule_ref":"27-multiple_microgrid_trading::v141-c-R003","node_id":"clearing.inter_area","function":"clearing.inter_area","unit_id":"27-multiple_microgrid_trading::v141-a-M01::clearing.inter_area","mechanism_id":"v141-a-M01","anchor":true},{"id":"27-multiple_microgrid_trading::v141-c-R010::V::clearing.inter_area","rule_id":"v141-c-R010","market_meaning":"Submitted neighboring energy requests provide inputs to the update of μG-i's intermicrogrid selling price. Scope: Iterative distributed trading between interconnected islanded microgrids. Condition: During each selling-price update using requests collected from neighboring microgrids. Exchange of the microgrids' demand bids precedes the regulation phase that adjusts intermicrogrid energy prices. Scope: The sequence within each iteration of the distributed intermicrogrid trading algorithm. Condition: Within each algorithm iteration. The locally updated offered energy quantity ε_i^{(s)}[k] is an input to μG-i's next selling-price update. Scope: Each μG-i's iterative selling-price update in distributed intermicrogrid trading. Condition: During each algorithm iteration after the local offered quantity has been updated. The intermicrogrid market-clearing process adjusts energy prices according to the law of demand until global demand matches offer. Scope: The distributed market-clearing phase for exchanges among interconnected islanded microgrids. Condition: During iterative clearing, until global energy demand matches the energy offer. The selling price must increase; the source states λ_i[k+1] ≥ λ_i[k]. Scope: The selling-price update at μG-i during distributed intermicrogrid clearing. Condition: The energy offered by μG-i is less than the total energy demanded from it by neighboring microgrids. μG-i's selling unit price equals its marginal generation cost. Each supplying μG-j's unit price equals μG-i's selling price minus the marginal transportation cost of the purchased energy. Scope: Optimal intermicrogrid prices in Case 6, where μG-i sells, buys, and generates. Condition: At the optimal solution λ^*, with j∈S_* for the supplying microgrids. μG-4 sells at a unit price given by its marginal generation cost and does not seek extra gains. Scope: μG-4 in the four-microgrid numerical example. Condition: After convergence, μG-4 operates in Case 4 (generates and sells), under the total-system-cost objective and the prohibition on cheating.","omega":{"market_type":"DEM","network_level":null,"jurisdiction":null,"pricing_scope":null,"time_scale":null,"participant_types":[],"trading_stage":"pricing","context_text":"Each μG-i's iterative selling-price update in distributed intermicrogrid trading. | Iterative distributed trading between interconnected islanded microgrids. | Optimal intermicrogrid prices in Case 6, where μG-i sells, buys, and generates. | The distributed market-clearing phase for exchanges among interconnected islanded microgrids. | The selling-price update at μG-i during distributed intermicrogrid clearing. | The sequence within each iteration of the distributed intermicrogrid trading algorithm. | μG-4 in the four-microgrid numerical example.","unknown_fields":["network_level","jurisdiction","pricing_scope","time_scale","participant_types"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e023","v141-b-g03-e003","v141-b-g03-e01","v141-b-g03-e06","v141-b-g03-e10","v141-b-g20-e020","v141-b-g22-e022","v141-b-g23-e023","v141-b-g24-e024","v141-b-g38-e038"]},"constraint_refs":["27-multiple_microgrid_trading::v141-c-R010::v141-c-R010-C004"],"decision_anchor":"pricing.inter_area","source_rule_sha256":"8755414fb2ed14dfa78514022cc0e4bdd20ebafcfee1a377920a8f924cb878c6","paper":"27-multiple_microgrid_trading","rule_ref":"27-multiple_microgrid_trading::v141-c-R010","node_id":"clearing.inter_area","function":"clearing.inter_area","unit_id":"27-multiple_microgrid_trading::v141-a-M01::clearing.inter_area","mechanism_id":"v141-a-M01","anchor":false}],"paper_ids":["04-energy_collectives","27-multiple_microgrid_trading"],"evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E29","location":{"location_id":"src-dfa28329fc-p05-c1r4__v141-a-M02-coordination","page":5,"printed_page":"3998","section":"III. The Role of Community Manager","anchor_text":"g p , p g , off- and connected to the grid [6], is only one possible conﬁg- w uration, but more gene","equation_id":null,"figure_id":"Fig. 2","table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"Energy collectives are to be envisaged as a market construct and not related to a certain positioning on the electric power grid. In practice, this means that operation on a micro-grid, both off- and connected to the grid [6], is only one possible configuration, but more generally such communities may be formed irrespective of grid connection and geographical location. The use of distribution (or transmission for longer distances) networks for community trades results in additional grid costs including several parameters, e.g. geographical distance and lines congestion. We include these additional costs by investigating a setup where an energy collective is formed by prosumers from three different neighbourhoods, N1, N2, N3. We model this system by splitting (1c) in three sub-communities and by defining energy flows, v = (v_12, v_13, v_23), on each line connecting the neighbourhoods, as depicted in Fig. 2. We include geographical differentiation as relative transaction costs gamma_geo = (gamma_geo,12, gamma_geo,13, gamma_geo,23).","paper":"04-energy_collectives","rule_id":"v141-c-R016","quote":"Energy collectives are to be envisaged as a market construct and not related to a certain positioning on the electric power grid. In practice, this means that operation on a micro-grid, both off- and connected to the grid [6], is only one possible configuration, but more generally such communities may be formed irrespective of grid connection and geographical location. The use of distribution (or transmission for longer distances) networks for community trades results in additional grid costs including several parameters, e.g. geographical distance and lines congestion. We include these additional costs by investigating a setup where an energy collective is formed by prosumers from three different neighbourhoods, N1, N2, N3. We model this system by splitting (1c) in three sub-communities and by defining energy flows, v = (v_12, v_13, v_23), on each line connecting the neighbourhoods, as depicted in Fig. 2. We include geographical differentiation as relative transaction costs gamma_geo = (gamma_geo,12, gamma_geo,13, gamma_geo,23)."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E30","location":{"location_id":"src-dfa28329fc-p05-c2r1__v141-a-M02-coordination","page":5,"printed_page":"3998","section":"III. The Role of Community Manager","anchor_text":"IEEE TRANSACTIONS ON POWER SYSTEMS, VOL. 34, NO. 5, SEPTEMBER 2019 γgeo = (γgeo,12, γgeo,13, γgeo,23","equation_id":"(12)","figure_id":"Fig. 2","table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"gamma_geo = (gamma_geo,12, gamma_geo,13, gamma_geo,23). It follows that  g(q_imp, q_exp) = gamma_imp q_imp + gamma_exp q_exp + gamma_geo^T |v|  sum_{j in N1} q_j = + v_12 + v_13  sum_{j in N2} q_j = - v_12 + v_23  sum_{j in N3} q_j = - v_13 - v_13   (12)  This formulation allows to represent technical constraints, typical of power flow analysis, in the form of spatial and temporal varying grid tariffs. This becomes fundamental in view of a possible integration of energy collectives in the current system operation. Furthermore, the collective can be fragmented to the extreme, i.e., to single prosumer, accounting for individual trades, as in [19]. In this way, the energy collective becomes a supervised version of a fully peer-to-peer economic dispatch.","paper":"04-energy_collectives","rule_id":"v141-c-R016","quote":"gamma_geo = (gamma_geo,12, gamma_geo,13, gamma_geo,23). It follows that  g(q_imp, q_exp) = gamma_imp q_imp + gamma_exp q_exp + gamma_geo^T |v|  sum_{j in N1} q_j = + v_12 + v_13  sum_{j in N2} q_j = - v_12 + v_23  sum_{j in N3} q_j = - v_13 - v_13   (12)  This formulation allows to represent technical constraints, typical of power flow analysis, in the form of spatial and temporal varying grid tariffs. This becomes fundamental in view of a possible integration of energy collectives in the current system operation. Furthermore, the collective can be fragmented to the extreme, i.e., to single prosumer, accounting for individual trades, as in [19]. In this way, the energy collective becomes a supervised version of a fully peer-to-peer economic dispatch."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e10","location":{"location_id":"src-dfa28329fc-p05-c2r1__v141-a-M02-coordination","page":5,"printed_page":"3998","section":"III. The Role of Community Manager","anchor_text":"IEEE TRANSACTIONS ON POWER SYSTEMS, VOL. 34, NO. 5, SEPTEMBER 2019 γgeo = (γgeo,12, γgeo,13, γgeo,23","equation_id":"(12)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"\\gamma_{geo} = (\\gamma_{geo,12}, \\gamma_{geo,13}, \\gamma_{geo,23}). It follows that\n\ng(q_{imp}, q_{exp}) = \\gamma_{imp}q_{imp} + \\gamma_{exp}q_{exp} + \\boldsymbol{\\gamma}_{geo}^{T}|\\boldsymbol{v}|\n\n\\sum_{j \\in N_1} q_j = + v_{12} + v_{13}\n\\sum_{j \\in N_2} q_j = - v_{12} + v_{23}\n\\sum_{j \\in N_3} q_j = - v_{13} - v_{13}                    (12)","paper":"04-energy_collectives","rule_id":"v141-c-R016","quote":"\\gamma_{geo} = (\\gamma_{geo,12}, \\gamma_{geo,13}, \\gamma_{geo,23}). It follows that\n\ng(q_{imp}, q_{exp}) = \\gamma_{imp}q_{imp} + \\gamma_{exp}q_{exp} + \\boldsymbol{\\gamma}_{geo}^{T}|\\boldsymbol{v}|\n\n\\sum_{j \\in N_1} q_j = + v_{12} + v_{13}\n\\sum_{j \\in N_2} q_j = - v_{12} + v_{23}\n\\sum_{j \\in N_3} q_j = - v_{13} - v_{13}                    (12)"},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e033","location":{"location_id":"src-f5dd785b87-p08-c2r4__v141-a-M01-bidding","page":8,"printed_page":null,"section":"V. Conclusion","anchor_text":"G 1 keeps local cost functions and local consumption private. More speciﬁcally, each algorithm itera","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-08.png","occurrence_role":"restatement"},"source_text":"More specifically, each algorithm iteration consists of a local mini-mization step followed by a market clearing process. During the first step, each microgrid computes its local energy bid and reveals it to its potential sellers.","paper":"27-multiple_microgrid_trading","rule_id":"v141-c-R003","quote":"More specifically, each algorithm iteration consists of a local mini-mization step followed by a market clearing process. During the first step, each microgrid computes its local energy bid and reveals it to its potential sellers."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g03-e003","location":{"location_id":"src-f5dd785b87-p02-c1r4__v141-a-M01-bidding","page":2,"printed_page":null,"section":"I. Introduction","anchor_text":"propose an algorithm based on dual decomposition that itera- tively solves the problem in a distribu","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-02.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"Interestingly, each iteration of the resulting algorithm has a straightforward interpretation in economical terms once the new operational variables we introduce are given the meaning of energy prices. First, each microgrid locally computes the amounts of energy it must produce, buy, and sell to minimize its local cost according to the current energy prices. Then, after exchanging the microgrid bids, a regulation phase follows, in which, in a distributed way, the energy prices are adjusted according to the law of demand. This two-step process iterates until a global agreement is reached about prices and transferred energies.","paper":"27-multiple_microgrid_trading","rule_id":"v141-c-R003","quote":"Interestingly, each iteration of the resulting algorithm has a straightforward interpretation in economical terms once the new operational variables we introduce are given the meaning of energy prices. First, each microgrid locally computes the amounts of energy it must produce, buy, and sell to minimize its local cost according to the current energy prices. Then, after exchanging the microgrid bids, a regulation phase follows, in which, in a distributed way, the energy prices are adjusted according to the law of demand. This two-step process iterates until a global agreement is reached about prices and transferred energies."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g22-e022","location":{"location_id":"src-f5dd785b87-p04-c1r3__v141-a-M01-coordination","page":4,"printed_page":null,"section":"III-A. Decentralizing the Problem","anchor_text":"μG-i initialize λi[0] repeat μGs exchange {λi[k]} sh μG-i computes ε(s)i [k] and E(b)i [k] by solvin","equation_id":"Algorithm 1","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"Algorithm 1 Distributed approach μG-i initialize λ_i[0] repeat μGs exchange {λ_i[k]} μG-i computes ε_i^{(s)}[k] and E_i^{(b)}[k] by solving (6) with fixed λ[k] μG-i informs μG-j, j≠i, about the energy it is willing to buy, namely E_{j,i}[k], at the given price λ_j[k] with energy requests E_{i,j}[k] from neighboring μGs, μG-i builds E_i^{(s)}[k] ← [E_{i,1}[k] ... E_{i,M}[k]]^T μG-i computes λ_i[k+1] ← λ_i[k] + α[k](e_i^T A E_i^{(s)}[k] - ε_i^{(s)}[k]) k ← k+1 until convergence condition is verified","paper":"27-multiple_microgrid_trading","rule_id":"v141-c-R003","quote":"Algorithm 1 Distributed approach μG-i initialize λ_i[0] repeat μGs exchange {λ_i[k]} μG-i computes ε_i^{(s)}[k] and E_i^{(b)}[k] by solving (6) with fixed λ[k] μG-i informs μG-j, j≠i, about the energy it is willing to buy, namely E_{j,i}[k], at the given price λ_j[k] with energy requests E_{i,j}[k] from neighboring μGs, μG-i builds E_i^{(s)}[k] ← [E_{i,1}[k] ... E_{i,M}[k]]^T μG-i computes λ_i[k+1] ← λ_i[k] + α[k](e_i^T A E_i^{(s)}[k] - ε_i^{(s)}[k]) k ← k+1 until convergence condition is verified"},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g24-e024","location":{"location_id":"src-f5dd785b87-p04-c1r4__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":4,"printed_page":null,"section":"III-A. Decentralizing the Problem","anchor_text":", g ining interpretation: Each Lagrange multiplier λi may be under- costood as the price per energy ","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"According to this view, updating step (8) is clearing the market, i.e., prices should be modified until, globally, the energy demand matches the energy offer. Note that (8) is an example of the law of demand: If the energy offered by μG-i ε_i^{(s)}[k] is less than all the energy demanded by the neighboring μGs from μG-i, i.e., e_i^T A E_i^{(s)}, then the selling price must increase, and λ_i[k+1] ≥ λ_i[k].","paper":"27-multiple_microgrid_trading","rule_id":"v141-c-R003","quote":"According to this view, updating step (8) is clearing the market, i.e., prices should be modified until, globally, the energy demand matches the energy offer. Note that (8) is an example of the law of demand: If the energy offered by μG-i ε_i^{(s)}[k] is less than all the energy demanded by the neighboring μGs from μG-i, i.e., e_i^T A E_i^{(s)}, then the selling price must increase, and λ_i[k+1] ≥ λ_i[k]."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g03-e12","location":{"location_id":"src-f5dd785b87-p08-c2r3__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":8,"printed_page":null,"section":"IV. Numerical Results","anchor_text":"local cost substantially. ost In Fig. 1(c), we depict another situation where the topology old heavi","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-08.png","occurrence_role":"example"},"source_text":"In this paper, we have addressed a problem in which several microgrids interact by exchanging energy in order to minimize the global operation cost while still satisfying their local demands.","paper":"27-multiple_microgrid_trading","rule_id":"v141-c-R003","quote":"In this paper, we have addressed a problem in which several microgrids interact by exchanging energy in order to minimize the global operation cost while still satisfying their local demands."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g08-e008","location":{"location_id":"src-f5dd785b87-p02-c2r4__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":2,"printed_page":null,"section":"II. System Model","anchor_text":"ra- Finally, we further assume that all μGs agree to cooperate gly, with one another in order to min","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-02.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"Finally, we further assume that all μGs agree to cooperate with one another in order to minimize the total cost of the system. In other words, the energy quantities exchanged by the interconnected μGs form the equilibrium point of the following minimization problem:","paper":"27-multiple_microgrid_trading","rule_id":"v141-c-R003","quote":"Finally, we further assume that all μGs agree to cooperate with one another in order to minimize the total cost of the system. In other words, the energy quantities exchanged by the interconnected μGs form the equilibrium point of the following minimization problem:"},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g15-e015","location":{"location_id":"src-f5dd785b87-p03-c2r2__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":3,"printed_page":null,"section":"III-A. Decentralizing the Problem","anchor_text":"i i i i i at {ε(s)i },{Ei,j} i=1 + eTi AT γ(E(b)i ) i=1 s.t. Ei,j ≥0 ∀i,j ks ey E(c)i + ε(s)i −eTi A","equation_id":"(4)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-03.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"C^* = min_{ {ε_i^{(s)}},{E_{i,j}} } ∑_{i=1}^M C_i(E_i^{(c)} + ε_i^{(s)} - e_i^T A^T E_i^{(b)}) + ∑_{i=1}^M e_i^T A^T γ(E_i^{(b)}) s.t. E_{i,j} ≥ 0 ∀i,j, E_i^{(c)} + ε_i^{(s)} - e_i^T A^T E_i^{(b)} ≥ 0 ∀i, ε_i^{(s)} = e_i^T A E_i^{(s)} ∀i. (4) The idea is that, for each μG, we first use the new variable ε_i^{(s)} to represent the energy sold by μG-i and only later force it to be equal to all the energy bought by other μGs from μG-i, i.e., ε_i^{(s)}=e_i^T A E_i^{(s)}, which is the coupling constraint.","paper":"27-multiple_microgrid_trading","rule_id":"v141-c-R003","quote":"C^* = min_{ {ε_i^{(s)}},{E_{i,j}} } ∑_{i=1}^M C_i(E_i^{(c)} + ε_i^{(s)} - e_i^T A^T E_i^{(b)}) + ∑_{i=1}^M e_i^T A^T γ(E_i^{(b)}) s.t. E_{i,j} ≥ 0 ∀i,j, E_i^{(c)} + ε_i^{(s)} - e_i^T A^T E_i^{(b)} ≥ 0 ∀i, ε_i^{(s)} = e_i^T A E_i^{(s)} ∀i. (4) The idea is that, for each μG, we first use the new variable ε_i^{(s)} to represent the energy sold by μG-i and only later force it to be equal to all the energy bought by other μGs from μG-i, i.e., ε_i^{(s)}=e_i^T A E_i^{(s)}, which is the coupling constraint."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g04-e004","location":{"location_id":"src-f5dd785b87-p02-c2r1__v141-a-M01-participation","page":2,"printed_page":null,"section":"II. System Model","anchor_text":"IEEE TRANSACTIONS ON INDUSTRIAL ELECTRONICS, VOL. 62, NO. 4, APRIL 2015 we from an economical point ","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-02.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"Consider a system composed of M interconnected microgrids (μGs) operating in islanded mode. During each scheduling interval, each microgrid μG-i generates E_i^{(g)} units of energy and consumes E_i^{(c)} units of energy. Moreover, μG-i may be allowed to sell energy E_{i,j} to μG-j, j≠i, and to buy energy E_{k,i} from μG-k, k≠i. Then, the energy equilibrium within the μG requires E_i^{(g)} + e_i^T A^T E_i^{(b)} = E_i^{(c)} + e_i^T A E_i^{(s)} (1)","paper":"27-multiple_microgrid_trading","rule_id":"v141-c-R003","quote":"Consider a system composed of M interconnected microgrids (μGs) operating in islanded mode. During each scheduling interval, each microgrid μG-i generates E_i^{(g)} units of energy and consumes E_i^{(c)} units of energy. Moreover, μG-i may be allowed to sell energy E_{i,j} to μG-j, j≠i, and to buy energy E_{k,i} from μG-k, k≠i. Then, the energy equilibrium within the μG requires E_i^{(g)} + e_i^T A^T E_i^{(b)} = E_i^{(c)} + e_i^T A E_i^{(s)} (1)"},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g16-e016","location":{"location_id":"src-f5dd785b87-p03-c2r3__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":3,"printed_page":null,"section":"III-A. Decentralizing the Problem","anchor_text":"we q y, ( )], y g the M coupling constraints and solving the following dual he problem: er C∗= max C","equation_id":"(5)-(6)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-03.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"C^* = max_λ C(λ) (5) where C(λ) = ∑_{i=1}^M C_i^{(l)}(λ) with the terms C_i^{(l)}(λ) = min_{ε_i^{(s)},E_i^{(b)}} C_i(ε_i^{(s)},E_i^{(b)},λ) s.t. ε_i^{(s)} ≥ 0, E_{j,i} ≥ 0 ∀j, E_i^{(c)} + ε_i^{(s)} - e_i^T A^T E_i^{(b)} ≥ 0. (6)","paper":"27-multiple_microgrid_trading","rule_id":"v141-c-R003","quote":"C^* = max_λ C(λ) (5) where C(λ) = ∑_{i=1}^M C_i^{(l)}(λ) with the terms C_i^{(l)}(λ) = min_{ε_i^{(s)},E_i^{(b)}} C_i(ε_i^{(s)},E_i^{(b)},λ) s.t. ε_i^{(s)} ≥ 0, E_{j,i} ≥ 0 ∀j, E_i^{(c)} + ε_i^{(s)} - e_i^T A^T E_i^{(b)} ≥ 0. (6)"},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g05-e005","location":{"location_id":"src-f5dd785b87-p02-c2r1__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":2,"printed_page":null,"section":"II. System Model","anchor_text":"IEEE TRANSACTIONS ON INDUSTRIAL ELECTRONICS, VOL. 62, NO. 4, APRIL 2015 we from an economical point ","equation_id":"(1)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-02.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"E_i^{(g)} + e_i^T A^T E_i^{(b)} = E_i^{(c)} + e_i^T A E_i^{(s)} (1)","paper":"27-multiple_microgrid_trading","rule_id":"v141-c-R003","quote":"E_i^{(g)} + e_i^T A^T E_i^{(b)} = E_i^{(c)} + e_i^T A E_i^{(s)} (1)"},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g06-e006","location":{"location_id":"src-f5dd785b87-p02-c2r2__v141-a-M01-participation","page":2,"printed_page":null,"section":"II. System Model","anchor_text":"to sell energy Ei,j to μG j, j̸ i, and to buy energy Ek,i fromrgy μG-k, k̸=i. Then, the energy equil","equation_id":"(2)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-02.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"where the two M-dimensional column vectors, i.e., E_i^{(b)} = [E_{1,i} ... E_{M,i}]^T, E_i^{(s)} = [E_{i,1} ... E_{i,M}]^T (2) gather all the energy quantities bought and sold, respectively, by μG-i. In addition, we have introduced adjacency matrix A = [a_{i,j}]_{M×M}, where element a_{i,j} is equal to 1 if there exists a connection from μG-i to μG-j and 0 otherwise. Note that, generally, A may be nonsymmetric, meaning that at least two μGs are allowed to exchange energy in one direction only. By convention, we fix a_{i,i}=0. Moreover, a_{i,j}=0 ⇒ E_{i,j}=0 MWh for all i,j=1,...,M.","paper":"27-multiple_microgrid_trading","rule_id":"v141-c-R003","quote":"where the two M-dimensional column vectors, i.e., E_i^{(b)} = [E_{1,i} ... E_{M,i}]^T, E_i^{(s)} = [E_{i,1} ... E_{i,M}]^T (2) gather all the energy quantities bought and sold, respectively, by μG-i. In addition, we have introduced adjacency matrix A = [a_{i,j}]_{M×M}, where element a_{i,j} is equal to 1 if there exists a connection from μG-i to μG-j and 0 otherwise. Note that, generally, A may be nonsymmetric, meaning that at least two μGs are allowed to exchange energy in one direction only. By convention, we fix a_{i,i}=0. Moreover, a_{i,j}=0 ⇒ E_{i,j}=0 MWh for all i,j=1,...,M."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g11-e011","location":{"location_id":"src-f5dd785b87-p03-c1r2__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":3,"printed_page":null,"section":"II-A. Cost Functions","anchor_text":"where achieving the global objective implies a cost reduction at every μG. A. Cost Functions As afor","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-03.png","occurrence_role":"condition"},"source_text":"As aforementioned, the algorithm proposed hereafter works with any set of generation/transfer cost functions as long as they all satisfy some mild convexity constraints. Specifically, it is required that {C_i(·)} and γ(·) are positive valued, monotonically increasing, convex, and twice differentiable. Although these requirements may seem abstract and distant from real systems, one should take into account that the cost function of a common electrical generator (e.g., oil, coal, nuclear,...) is often modeled as a quadratic polynomial C(x)=a+bx+cx^2, where coefficients a, b, and c depend on the generator type (see [30] and particularly [31]). Such a generation model clearly satisfies our assumptions, and without available counterexamples, we extended it to the transfer cost function γ(·).","paper":"27-multiple_microgrid_trading","rule_id":"v141-c-R003","quote":"As aforementioned, the algorithm proposed hereafter works with any set of generation/transfer cost functions as long as they all satisfy some mild convexity constraints. Specifically, it is required that {C_i(·)} and γ(·) are positive valued, monotonically increasing, convex, and twice differentiable. Although these requirements may seem abstract and distant from real systems, one should take into account that the cost function of a common electrical generator (e.g., oil, coal, nuclear,...) is often modeled as a quadratic polynomial C(x)=a+bx+cx^2, where coefficients a, b, and c depend on the generator type (see [30] and particularly [31]). Such a generation model clearly satisfies our assumptions, and without available counterexamples, we extended it to the transfer cost function γ(·)."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g12-e012","location":{"location_id":"src-f5dd785b87-p03-c1r3__v141-a-M01-participation","page":3,"printed_page":null,"section":"II-A. Cost Functions","anchor_text":"our assumptions, and without available counterexamples, we q extended it to the transfer cost functi","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-03.png","occurrence_role":"exception"},"source_text":"It is worth commenting here that the assumptions on the cost functions also allow for a simple way to introduce upper bounds on the energy generated by the μGs or supported by the transfer connections. Indeed, one can introduce soft constraints by designing the cost function with a steep rise at the nominal maximum value (see also the first paragraph in Section IV, where we comment on the cost functions used for simulation). By doing so, the maximum generated/transferred energy quantities are directly controlled by the cost functions without the need for hard constraints (i.e., well-specified inequalities such as E_i^{(g)} ≤ E_{i,max}^{(g)}) that would increase the complexity of the minimization problem in (3). Note that this expedient translates, somehow, to a more flexible system, i.e., when needed, a μG can produce more energy than the nominal maximum if it is willing to pay an (significant) extra cost. Indeed, this situation arises in practical systems when backup generators are activated.","paper":"27-multiple_microgrid_trading","rule_id":"v141-c-R003","quote":"It is worth commenting here that the assumptions on the cost functions also allow for a simple way to introduce upper bounds on the energy generated by the μGs or supported by the transfer connections. Indeed, one can introduce soft constraints by designing the cost function with a steep rise at the nominal maximum value (see also the first paragraph in Section IV, where we comment on the cost functions used for simulation). By doing so, the maximum generated/transferred energy quantities are directly controlled by the cost functions without the need for hard constraints (i.e., well-specified inequalities such as E_i^{(g)} ≤ E_{i,max}^{(g)}) that would increase the complexity of the minimization problem in (3). Note that this expedient translates, somehow, to a more flexible system, i.e., when needed, a μG can produce more energy than the nominal maximum if it is willing to pay an (significant) extra cost. Indeed, this situation arises in practical systems when backup generators are activated."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g03-e10","location":{"location_id":"src-f5dd785b87-p08-c2r4__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":8,"printed_page":null,"section":"V. Conclusion","anchor_text":"G 1 keeps local cost functions and local consumption private. More speciﬁcally, each algorithm itera","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-08.png","occurrence_role":"restatement"},"source_text":"Next, during the market clearing process, energy prices are adjusted according to the law of demand.","paper":"27-multiple_microgrid_trading","rule_id":"v141-c-R010","quote":"Next, during the market clearing process, energy prices are adjusted according to the law of demand."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g24-e024","location":{"location_id":"src-f5dd785b87-p04-c1r4__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":4,"printed_page":null,"section":"III-A. Decentralizing the Problem","anchor_text":", g ining interpretation: Each Lagrange multiplier λi may be under- costood as the price per energy ","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"According to this view, updating step (8) is clearing the market, i.e., prices should be modified until, globally, the energy demand matches the energy offer. Note that (8) is an example of the law of demand: If the energy offered by μG-i ε_i^{(s)}[k] is less than all the energy demanded by the neighboring μGs from μG-i, i.e., e_i^T A E_i^{(s)}, then the selling price must increase, and λ_i[k+1] ≥ λ_i[k].","paper":"27-multiple_microgrid_trading","rule_id":"v141-c-R010","quote":"According to this view, updating step (8) is clearing the market, i.e., prices should be modified until, globally, the energy demand matches the energy offer. Note that (8) is an example of the law of demand: If the energy offered by μG-i ε_i^{(s)}[k] is less than all the energy demanded by the neighboring μGs from μG-i, i.e., e_i^T A E_i^{(s)}, then the selling price must increase, and λ_i[k+1] ≥ λ_i[k]."}],"candidate_only":true,"classification_subtypes":["geography_weighted_flow_allocation","joint_cost_minimizing_allocation"],"projection_statuses":["clear"],"taxonomy":{"primary_family":"kind-L5-c2715d2926","secondary_families":[],"stage":"L5","ontological_kind":"geography_weighted_flow_allocation"},"semantic_definition":{"definition":"Through a source-explicit regional balance relation, associate regional net exchange with cross-regional directed flows, and select the cross-regional configuration under an objective that includes interconnection flow transaction costs.","object":"Inter-regional interconnection flows and their coupled regional net exchange and generation-consumption configuration.","inputs":"Regional supply-demand or net-exchange information, interconnection representation, and flow cost rules; external exchange charges, generation costs, and interconnection permissions are provided according to the source.","operation":"Aggregate the associated regional net exchange using signed interconnection flows, include each interconnection's flow cost in configuration selection, and satisfy the quantity and coupling conditions specified by the source.","outputs":"Cross-regional flows satisfying the specified regional balance, and the related net exchange and production configuration.","boundary":"Limited to regional flow configuration in which L5/JOC costs participate; an overall cost objective or internal zero-sum alone is insufficient for inclusion. Retain the original balance signs, cost functions, and quantity domains; do not complete unsupported balance equations; do not change soft capacity into a hard upper limit."},"source_units":[{"unit_id":"04-energy_collectives::v141-a-M02::clearing.inter_area","paper_id":"04-energy_collectives","mechanism_id":"v141-a-M02","function":"clearing.inter_area","source_occurrences":[{"id":"04-energy_collectives::v141-c-R016::V::clearing.inter_area","rule_id":"v141-c-R016","market_meaning":"The inter-neighbourhood allocation model includes relative geographical transaction costs weighted by the magnitudes of energy flows on the connecting lines. Scope: Inter-neighbourhood allocations within the illustrated three-neighbourhood energy collective. Condition: When the geographical-preference formulation is adopted. Inter-neighbourhood allocations must satisfy the specified balances for N1 and N2: each neighbourhood's aggregate community exchange equals the signed sum of its connecting-line flows. Scope: The N1 and N2 balance restrictions in the illustrated collective comprising neighbourhoods N1, N2 and N3. Condition: When the three-neighbourhood geographical-preference formulation is adopted.","omega":{"market_type":"LEM_DEM","network_level":null,"jurisdiction":null,"pricing_scope":null,"time_scale":null,"participant_types":[],"trading_stage":"clearing","context_text":"Inter-neighbourhood allocations within the illustrated three-neighbourhood energy collective. | The N1 and N2 balance restrictions in the illustrated collective comprising neighbourhoods N1, N2 and N3.","unknown_fields":["network_level","jurisdiction","pricing_scope","time_scale","participant_types"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E29","v141-b-g01-E30"]},"constraint_refs":["04-energy_collectives::v141-c-R016::v141-c-R016-C001","04-energy_collectives::v141-c-R016::v141-c-R016-C002"],"decision_anchor":"clearing.inter_area","source_rule_sha256":"832badce48c31455b3a33e4e3257f117b85ec95e34b8d1b1304beda2d487009e"}],"constraints":[{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R016-C001","kind":"mathematical","role":"core","statement":"The inter-neighbourhood allocation model includes relative geographical transaction costs weighted by the magnitudes of energy flows on the connecting lines. Scope: Inter-neighbourhood allocations within the illustrated three-neighbourhood energy collective. Condition: When the geographical-preference formulation is adopted.","expression":"g(q_{imp}, q_{exp}) = \\gamma_{imp} q_{imp} + \\gamma_{exp} q_{exp} + \\gamma_{geo}^{\\top} |\\boldsymbol{v}|","formalization_origin":"source","template_id":null,"members":["clearing.inter_area"],"variable_refs":["v141-c-R016-x-qimp","v141-c-R016-x-qexp","v141-c-R016-x-v"],"parameter_refs":["v141-c-R016-theta-gamma-imp","v141-c-R016-theta-gamma-exp","v141-c-R016-theta-gamma-geo"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E29","v141-b-g01-E30","v141-b-g02-e10"],"ref":"04-energy_collectives::v141-c-R016::v141-c-R016-C001","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R016","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E29","location":{"location_id":"src-dfa28329fc-p05-c1r4__v141-a-M02-coordination","page":5,"printed_page":"3998","section":"III. The Role of Community Manager","anchor_text":"g p , p g , off- and connected to the grid [6], is only one possible conﬁg- w uration, but more gene","equation_id":null,"figure_id":"Fig. 2","table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"Energy collectives are to be envisaged as a market construct and not related to a certain positioning on the electric power grid. In practice, this means that operation on a micro-grid, both off- and connected to the grid [6], is only one possible configuration, but more generally such communities may be formed irrespective of grid connection and geographical location. The use of distribution (or transmission for longer distances) networks for community trades results in additional grid costs including several parameters, e.g. geographical distance and lines congestion. We include these additional costs by investigating a setup where an energy collective is formed by prosumers from three different neighbourhoods, N1, N2, N3. We model this system by splitting (1c) in three sub-communities and by defining energy flows, v = (v_12, v_13, v_23), on each line connecting the neighbourhoods, as depicted in Fig. 2. We include geographical differentiation as relative transaction costs gamma_geo = (gamma_geo,12, gamma_geo,13, gamma_geo,23)."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E30","location":{"location_id":"src-dfa28329fc-p05-c2r1__v141-a-M02-coordination","page":5,"printed_page":"3998","section":"III. The Role of Community Manager","anchor_text":"IEEE TRANSACTIONS ON POWER SYSTEMS, VOL. 34, NO. 5, SEPTEMBER 2019 γgeo = (γgeo,12, γgeo,13, γgeo,23","equation_id":"(12)","figure_id":"Fig. 2","table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"gamma_geo = (gamma_geo,12, gamma_geo,13, gamma_geo,23). It follows that  g(q_imp, q_exp) = gamma_imp q_imp + gamma_exp q_exp + gamma_geo^T |v|  sum_{j in N1} q_j = + v_12 + v_13  sum_{j in N2} q_j = - v_12 + v_23  sum_{j in N3} q_j = - v_13 - v_13   (12)  This formulation allows to represent technical constraints, typical of power flow analysis, in the form of spatial and temporal varying grid tariffs. This becomes fundamental in view of a possible integration of energy collectives in the current system operation. Furthermore, the collective can be fragmented to the extreme, i.e., to single prosumer, accounting for individual trades, as in [19]. In this way, the energy collective becomes a supervised version of a fully peer-to-peer economic dispatch."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e10","location":{"location_id":"src-dfa28329fc-p05-c2r1__v141-a-M02-coordination","page":5,"printed_page":"3998","section":"III. The Role of Community Manager","anchor_text":"IEEE TRANSACTIONS ON POWER SYSTEMS, VOL. 34, NO. 5, SEPTEMBER 2019 γgeo = (γgeo,12, γgeo,13, γgeo,23","equation_id":"(12)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"\\gamma_{geo} = (\\gamma_{geo,12}, \\gamma_{geo,13}, \\gamma_{geo,23}). It follows that\n\ng(q_{imp}, q_{exp}) = \\gamma_{imp}q_{imp} + \\gamma_{exp}q_{exp} + \\boldsymbol{\\gamma}_{geo}^{T}|\\boldsymbol{v}|\n\n\\sum_{j \\in N_1} q_j = + v_{12} + v_{13}\n\\sum_{j \\in N_2} q_j = - v_{12} + v_{23}\n\\sum_{j \\in N_3} q_j = - v_{13} - v_{13}                    (12)"}]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R016-C002","kind":"mathematical","role":"core","statement":"Inter-neighbourhood allocations must satisfy the specified balances for N1 and N2: each neighbourhood's aggregate community exchange equals the signed sum of its connecting-line flows. Scope: The N1 and N2 balance restrictions in the illustrated collective comprising neighbourhoods N1, N2 and N3. Condition: When the three-neighbourhood geographical-preference formulation is adopted.","expression":"\\sum_{j \\in N_1} q_j = + v_{12} + v_{13},\\quad \\sum_{j \\in N_2} q_j = - v_{12} + v_{23}","formalization_origin":"source","template_id":null,"members":["clearing.inter_area"],"variable_refs":["v141-c-R016-x-qj","v141-c-R016-x-v12","v141-c-R016-x-v13","v141-c-R016-x-v23"],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E29","v141-b-g01-E30","v141-b-g02-e10"],"ref":"04-energy_collectives::v141-c-R016::v141-c-R016-C002","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R016","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E29","location":{"location_id":"src-dfa28329fc-p05-c1r4__v141-a-M02-coordination","page":5,"printed_page":"3998","section":"III. The Role of Community Manager","anchor_text":"g p , p g , off- and connected to the grid [6], is only one possible conﬁg- w uration, but more gene","equation_id":null,"figure_id":"Fig. 2","table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"Energy collectives are to be envisaged as a market construct and not related to a certain positioning on the electric power grid. In practice, this means that operation on a micro-grid, both off- and connected to the grid [6], is only one possible configuration, but more generally such communities may be formed irrespective of grid connection and geographical location. The use of distribution (or transmission for longer distances) networks for community trades results in additional grid costs including several parameters, e.g. geographical distance and lines congestion. We include these additional costs by investigating a setup where an energy collective is formed by prosumers from three different neighbourhoods, N1, N2, N3. We model this system by splitting (1c) in three sub-communities and by defining energy flows, v = (v_12, v_13, v_23), on each line connecting the neighbourhoods, as depicted in Fig. 2. We include geographical differentiation as relative transaction costs gamma_geo = (gamma_geo,12, gamma_geo,13, gamma_geo,23)."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E30","location":{"location_id":"src-dfa28329fc-p05-c2r1__v141-a-M02-coordination","page":5,"printed_page":"3998","section":"III. The Role of Community Manager","anchor_text":"IEEE TRANSACTIONS ON POWER SYSTEMS, VOL. 34, NO. 5, SEPTEMBER 2019 γgeo = (γgeo,12, γgeo,13, γgeo,23","equation_id":"(12)","figure_id":"Fig. 2","table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"gamma_geo = (gamma_geo,12, gamma_geo,13, gamma_geo,23). It follows that  g(q_imp, q_exp) = gamma_imp q_imp + gamma_exp q_exp + gamma_geo^T |v|  sum_{j in N1} q_j = + v_12 + v_13  sum_{j in N2} q_j = - v_12 + v_23  sum_{j in N3} q_j = - v_13 - v_13   (12)  This formulation allows to represent technical constraints, typical of power flow analysis, in the form of spatial and temporal varying grid tariffs. This becomes fundamental in view of a possible integration of energy collectives in the current system operation. Furthermore, the collective can be fragmented to the extreme, i.e., to single prosumer, accounting for individual trades, as in [19]. In this way, the energy collective becomes a supervised version of a fully peer-to-peer economic dispatch."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e10","location":{"location_id":"src-dfa28329fc-p05-c2r1__v141-a-M02-coordination","page":5,"printed_page":"3998","section":"III. The Role of Community Manager","anchor_text":"IEEE TRANSACTIONS ON POWER SYSTEMS, VOL. 34, NO. 5, SEPTEMBER 2019 γgeo = (γgeo,12, γgeo,13, γgeo,23","equation_id":"(12)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"\\gamma_{geo} = (\\gamma_{geo,12}, \\gamma_{geo,13}, \\gamma_{geo,23}). It follows that\n\ng(q_{imp}, q_{exp}) = \\gamma_{imp}q_{imp} + \\gamma_{exp}q_{exp} + \\boldsymbol{\\gamma}_{geo}^{T}|\\boldsymbol{v}|\n\n\\sum_{j \\in N_1} q_j = + v_{12} + v_{13}\n\\sum_{j \\in N_2} q_j = - v_{12} + v_{23}\n\\sum_{j \\in N_3} q_j = - v_{13} - v_{13}                    (12)"}]}],"variables":[{"variable_id":"v141-c-R016-x-qj","symbol":"q_j","meaning":"Energy imported by prosumer j from the community (community-level exchange quantity), used in the neighbourhood balance sums.","kind":"operational","domain":"unspecified (source lists q_j among decision variables in the exchange problem; no explicit domain/bounds in the supplied evidence)","unit":null,"indices":["j = 1, ..., n","j belongs to N_1, N_2 or N_3"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E30","v141-b-g02-e10"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R016","declaration_ref":"04-energy_collectives::v141-c-R016::x::v141-c-R016-x-qj"},{"variable_id":"v141-c-R016-x-v","symbol":"\\boldsymbol{v}","meaning":"Vector of directed energy flows on the lines connecting the neighbourhoods: v = (v_{12}, v_{13}, v_{23}).","kind":"operational","domain":"unspecified (source defines v as energy flows; no explicit domain in the supplied evidence)","unit":null,"indices":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E29","v141-b-g01-E30","v141-b-g02-e09","v141-b-g02-e10"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R016","declaration_ref":"04-energy_collectives::v141-c-R016::x::v141-c-R016-x-v"},{"variable_id":"v141-c-R016-x-v12","symbol":"v_{12}","meaning":"Directed energy flow on the line connecting neighbourhoods N_1 and N_2.","kind":"operational","domain":"unspecified (source defines v_12 as an energy flow; no explicit domain in the supplied evidence)","unit":null,"indices":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E29","v141-b-g01-E30","v141-b-g02-e09","v141-b-g02-e10"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R016","declaration_ref":"04-energy_collectives::v141-c-R016::x::v141-c-R016-x-v12"},{"variable_id":"v141-c-R016-x-v13","symbol":"v_{13}","meaning":"Directed energy flow on the line connecting neighbourhoods N_1 and N_3.","kind":"operational","domain":"unspecified (source defines v_13 as an energy flow; no explicit domain in the supplied evidence)","unit":null,"indices":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E29","v141-b-g01-E30","v141-b-g02-e09","v141-b-g02-e10"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R016","declaration_ref":"04-energy_collectives::v141-c-R016::x::v141-c-R016-x-v13"},{"variable_id":"v141-c-R016-x-v23","symbol":"v_{23}","meaning":"Directed energy flow on the line connecting neighbourhoods N_2 and N_3.","kind":"operational","domain":"unspecified (source defines v_23 as an energy flow; no explicit domain in the supplied evidence)","unit":null,"indices":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E29","v141-b-g01-E30","v141-b-g02-e09","v141-b-g02-e10"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R016","declaration_ref":"04-energy_collectives::v141-c-R016::x::v141-c-R016-x-v23"},{"variable_id":"v141-c-R016-x-qimp","symbol":"q_{imp}","meaning":"Total energy imported by the community from the market or system operator, appearing in the geographical-preference interface objective g.","kind":"operational","domain":"unspecified (source uses q_imp as a global variable in g; no explicit domain in the supplied evidence for this rule)","unit":null,"indices":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E30","v141-b-g02-e10"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R016","declaration_ref":"04-energy_collectives::v141-c-R016::x::v141-c-R016-x-qimp"},{"variable_id":"v141-c-R016-x-qexp","symbol":"q_{exp}","meaning":"Total energy exported by the community to the market or system operator, appearing in the geographical-preference interface objective g.","kind":"operational","domain":"unspecified (source uses q_exp as a global variable in g; no explicit domain in the supplied evidence for this rule)","unit":null,"indices":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E30","v141-b-g02-e10"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R016","declaration_ref":"04-energy_collectives::v141-c-R016::x::v141-c-R016-x-qexp"}],"parameters":[{"parameter_id":"v141-c-R016-theta-gamma-geo","symbol":"\\gamma_{geo}","meaning":"Vector of relative geographical transaction costs between neighbourhoods: (\\gamma_{geo,12}, \\gamma_{geo,13}, \\gamma_{geo,23}).","value":{"kind":"unspecified","number_value":null,"text_value":null},"unit":null,"indices":["12, 13, 23"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E29","v141-b-g01-E30","v141-b-g02-e09","v141-b-g02-e10"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R016","declaration_ref":"04-energy_collectives::v141-c-R016::theta::v141-c-R016-theta-gamma-geo"},{"parameter_id":"v141-c-R016-theta-gamma-imp","symbol":"\\gamma_{imp}","meaning":"Penalty factor / price the community is willing to pay for importing energy from the market or system operator; it multiplies q_imp in the geographical-preference interface objective.","value":{"kind":"unspecified","number_value":null,"text_value":null},"unit":null,"indices":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E30","v141-b-g02-e10"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R016","declaration_ref":"04-energy_collectives::v141-c-R016::theta::v141-c-R016-theta-gamma-imp"},{"parameter_id":"v141-c-R016-theta-gamma-exp","symbol":"\\gamma_{exp}","meaning":"Penalty factor / price the community is willing to receive for exporting energy to the market or system operator; it multiplies q_exp in the geographical-preference interface objective.","value":{"kind":"unspecified","number_value":null,"text_value":null},"unit":null,"indices":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E30","v141-b-g02-e10"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R016","declaration_ref":"04-energy_collectives::v141-c-R016::theta::v141-c-R016-theta-gamma-exp"}]},{"unit_id":"27-multiple_microgrid_trading::v141-a-M01::clearing.inter_area","paper_id":"27-multiple_microgrid_trading","mechanism_id":"v141-a-M01","function":"clearing.inter_area","source_occurrences":[{"id":"27-multiple_microgrid_trading::v141-c-R003::V::clearing.inter_area","rule_id":"v141-c-R003","market_meaning":"Computation and disclosure of a microgrid's purchase bid to potential sellers precede the inter-microgrid market-clearing process. Scope: Each iteration of distributed energy trading among interconnected islanded microgrids. Local computation of the energy quantity offered for sale precedes the distributed price-adjustment phase identified as inter-microgrid market clearing. Scope: Each iteration of distributed energy trading among interconnected islanded microgrids. Exchanged energy is allocated to minimize global operating cost while satisfying the microgrids' local demands. Scope: System-level allocation of energy exchanged among interconnected islanded microgrids. Condition: All microgrids agree to cooperate to minimize total system cost. Each microgrid's generation plus imports must equal consumption plus exports. Local subproblems constrain offered sales, purchases and residual generation to be nonnegative. Scope: Feasible inter-microgrid allocations and the local optimization subproblems used to compute them. The aggregate energy sold by each microgrid must equal the total energy bought from it by the other microgrids; this coupling constraint governs feasible allocations rather than requiring agreement at every intermediate iteration. Scope: Feasible solutions of the reformulated inter-microgrid allocation problem (4). The allocated energy transfer E_{i,j} must be zero. Scope: Directed inter-microgrid transfer allocations. Condition: There is no directed connection from μG-i to μG-j, so a_{i,j}=0. All generation and transfer cost functions must be positive valued, monotonically increasing, convex and twice differentiable. Scope: Applicability of the proposed distributed inter-microgrid cost-minimization method. Nominal generation and link-transfer bounds may be represented by steeply rising cost functions. A microgrid may exceed its nominal generation maximum at significant additional cost. Scope: Optional representation of nominal generation and transfer-capacity bounds in the inter-microgrid allocation problem. Condition: Nominal upper bounds are introduced through cost-function soft constraints.","omega":{"market_type":"DEM","network_level":null,"jurisdiction":null,"pricing_scope":null,"time_scale":null,"participant_types":[],"trading_stage":"clearing","context_text":"Applicability of the proposed distributed inter-microgrid cost-minimization method. | Directed inter-microgrid transfer allocations. | Each iteration of distributed energy trading among interconnected islanded microgrids. | Feasible inter-microgrid allocations and the local optimization subproblems used to compute them. | Feasible solutions of the reformulated inter-microgrid allocation problem (4). | Optional representation of nominal generation and transfer-capacity bounds in the inter-microgrid allocation problem. | System-level allocation of energy exchanged among interconnected islanded microgrids.","unknown_fields":["network_level","jurisdiction","pricing_scope","time_scale","participant_types"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e033","v141-b-g03-e003","v141-b-g03-e12","v141-b-g04-e004","v141-b-g06-e006","v141-b-g08-e008","v141-b-g11-e011","v141-b-g12-e012","v141-b-g15-e015","v141-b-g16-e016","v141-b-g22-e022","v141-b-g24-e024"]},"constraint_refs":["27-multiple_microgrid_trading::v141-c-R003::v141-c-R003-C001","27-multiple_microgrid_trading::v141-c-R003::v141-c-R003-C002","27-multiple_microgrid_trading::v141-c-R003::v141-c-R003-C003","27-multiple_microgrid_trading::v141-c-R003::v141-c-R003-C004","27-multiple_microgrid_trading::v141-c-R003::v141-c-R003-C005","27-multiple_microgrid_trading::v141-c-R003::v141-c-R003-C006","27-multiple_microgrid_trading::v141-c-R003::v141-c-R003-C007","27-multiple_microgrid_trading::v141-c-R003::v141-c-R003-C008"],"decision_anchor":"clearing.inter_area","source_rule_sha256":"c4fac6566f29b61465c31094a8d1014b0e576fa9ad359b563b0d945606e255a2"},{"id":"27-multiple_microgrid_trading::v141-c-R010::V::clearing.inter_area","rule_id":"v141-c-R010","market_meaning":"Submitted neighboring energy requests provide inputs to the update of μG-i's intermicrogrid selling price. Scope: Iterative distributed trading between interconnected islanded microgrids. Condition: During each selling-price update using requests collected from neighboring microgrids. Exchange of the microgrids' demand bids precedes the regulation phase that adjusts intermicrogrid energy prices. Scope: The sequence within each iteration of the distributed intermicrogrid trading algorithm. Condition: Within each algorithm iteration. The locally updated offered energy quantity ε_i^{(s)}[k] is an input to μG-i's next selling-price update. Scope: Each μG-i's iterative selling-price update in distributed intermicrogrid trading. Condition: During each algorithm iteration after the local offered quantity has been updated. The intermicrogrid market-clearing process adjusts energy prices according to the law of demand until global demand matches offer. Scope: The distributed market-clearing phase for exchanges among interconnected islanded microgrids. Condition: During iterative clearing, until global energy demand matches the energy offer. The selling price must increase; the source states λ_i[k+1] ≥ λ_i[k]. Scope: The selling-price update at μG-i during distributed intermicrogrid clearing. Condition: The energy offered by μG-i is less than the total energy demanded from it by neighboring microgrids. μG-i's selling unit price equals its marginal generation cost. Each supplying μG-j's unit price equals μG-i's selling price minus the marginal transportation cost of the purchased energy. Scope: Optimal intermicrogrid prices in Case 6, where μG-i sells, buys, and generates. Condition: At the optimal solution λ^*, with j∈S_* for the supplying microgrids. μG-4 sells at a unit price given by its marginal generation cost and does not seek extra gains. Scope: μG-4 in the four-microgrid numerical example. Condition: After convergence, μG-4 operates in Case 4 (generates and sells), under the total-system-cost objective and the prohibition on cheating.","omega":{"market_type":"DEM","network_level":null,"jurisdiction":null,"pricing_scope":null,"time_scale":null,"participant_types":[],"trading_stage":"pricing","context_text":"Each μG-i's iterative selling-price update in distributed intermicrogrid trading. | Iterative distributed trading between interconnected islanded microgrids. | Optimal intermicrogrid prices in Case 6, where μG-i sells, buys, and generates. | The distributed market-clearing phase for exchanges among interconnected islanded microgrids. | The selling-price update at μG-i during distributed intermicrogrid clearing. | The sequence within each iteration of the distributed intermicrogrid trading algorithm. | μG-4 in the four-microgrid numerical example.","unknown_fields":["network_level","jurisdiction","pricing_scope","time_scale","participant_types"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e023","v141-b-g03-e003","v141-b-g03-e01","v141-b-g03-e06","v141-b-g03-e10","v141-b-g20-e020","v141-b-g22-e022","v141-b-g23-e023","v141-b-g24-e024","v141-b-g38-e038"]},"constraint_refs":["27-multiple_microgrid_trading::v141-c-R010::v141-c-R010-C004"],"decision_anchor":"pricing.inter_area","source_rule_sha256":"8755414fb2ed14dfa78514022cc0e4bdd20ebafcfee1a377920a8f924cb878c6"}],"constraints":[{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R003-C001","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"Computation and disclosure of a microgrid's purchase bid to potential sellers precede the inter-microgrid market-clearing process. Scope: Each iteration of distributed energy trading among interconnected islanded microgrids.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["bidding.demand","clearing.inter_area"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e033"],"ref":"27-multiple_microgrid_trading::v141-c-R003::v141-c-R003-C001","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R003","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e033","location":{"location_id":"src-f5dd785b87-p08-c2r4__v141-a-M01-bidding","page":8,"printed_page":null,"section":"V. Conclusion","anchor_text":"G 1 keeps local cost functions and local consumption private. More speciﬁcally, each algorithm itera","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-08.png","occurrence_role":"restatement"},"source_text":"More specifically, each algorithm iteration consists of a local mini-mization step followed by a market clearing process. During the first step, each microgrid computes its local energy bid and reveals it to its potential sellers."}]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R003-C002","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"Local computation of the energy quantity offered for sale precedes the distributed price-adjustment phase identified as inter-microgrid market clearing. Scope: Each iteration of distributed energy trading among interconnected islanded microgrids.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["bidding.supply","clearing.inter_area"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g03-e003","v141-b-g22-e022","v141-b-g24-e024"],"ref":"27-multiple_microgrid_trading::v141-c-R003::v141-c-R003-C002","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R003","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g03-e003","location":{"location_id":"src-f5dd785b87-p02-c1r4__v141-a-M01-bidding","page":2,"printed_page":null,"section":"I. Introduction","anchor_text":"propose an algorithm based on dual decomposition that itera- tively solves the problem in a distribu","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-02.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"Interestingly, each iteration of the resulting algorithm has a straightforward interpretation in economical terms once the new operational variables we introduce are given the meaning of energy prices. First, each microgrid locally computes the amounts of energy it must produce, buy, and sell to minimize its local cost according to the current energy prices. Then, after exchanging the microgrid bids, a regulation phase follows, in which, in a distributed way, the energy prices are adjusted according to the law of demand. This two-step process iterates until a global agreement is reached about prices and transferred energies."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g22-e022","location":{"location_id":"src-f5dd785b87-p04-c1r3__v141-a-M01-coordination","page":4,"printed_page":null,"section":"III-A. Decentralizing the Problem","anchor_text":"μG-i initialize λi[0] repeat μGs exchange {λi[k]} sh μG-i computes ε(s)i [k] and E(b)i [k] by solvin","equation_id":"Algorithm 1","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"Algorithm 1 Distributed approach μG-i initialize λ_i[0] repeat μGs exchange {λ_i[k]} μG-i computes ε_i^{(s)}[k] and E_i^{(b)}[k] by solving (6) with fixed λ[k] μG-i informs μG-j, j≠i, about the energy it is willing to buy, namely E_{j,i}[k], at the given price λ_j[k] with energy requests E_{i,j}[k] from neighboring μGs, μG-i builds E_i^{(s)}[k] ← [E_{i,1}[k] ... E_{i,M}[k]]^T μG-i computes λ_i[k+1] ← λ_i[k] + α[k](e_i^T A E_i^{(s)}[k] - ε_i^{(s)}[k]) k ← k+1 until convergence condition is verified"},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g24-e024","location":{"location_id":"src-f5dd785b87-p04-c1r4__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":4,"printed_page":null,"section":"III-A. Decentralizing the Problem","anchor_text":", g ining interpretation: Each Lagrange multiplier λi may be under- costood as the price per energy ","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"According to this view, updating step (8) is clearing the market, i.e., prices should be modified until, globally, the energy demand matches the energy offer. Note that (8) is an example of the law of demand: If the energy offered by μG-i ε_i^{(s)}[k] is less than all the energy demanded by the neighboring μGs from μG-i, i.e., e_i^T A E_i^{(s)}, then the selling price must increase, and λ_i[k+1] ≥ λ_i[k]."}]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R003-C003","kind":"mathematical","role":"core","statement":"Exchanged energy is allocated to minimize global operating cost while satisfying the microgrids' local demands. Scope: System-level allocation of energy exchanged among interconnected islanded microgrids. Condition: All microgrids agree to cooperate to minimize total system cost.","expression":"C^* = min_{ {ε_i^{(s)}},{E_{i,j}} } ∑_{i=1}^M C_i(E_i^{(c)} + ε_i^{(s)} - e_i^T A^T E_i^{(b)}) + ∑_{i=1}^M e_i^T A^T γ(E_i^{(b)})","formalization_origin":"source","template_id":null,"members":["clearing.inter_area"],"variable_refs":["v141-c-R003-x-eps","v141-c-R003-x-Eij","v141-c-R003-x-Eb"],"parameter_refs":["v141-c-R003-theta-M","v141-c-R003-theta-Ec","v141-c-R003-theta-A","v141-c-R003-theta-ei","v141-c-R003-theta-Ci","v141-c-R003-theta-gamma"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g03-e12","v141-b-g08-e008","v141-b-g15-e015"],"ref":"27-multiple_microgrid_trading::v141-c-R003::v141-c-R003-C003","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R003","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g03-e12","location":{"location_id":"src-f5dd785b87-p08-c2r3__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":8,"printed_page":null,"section":"IV. Numerical Results","anchor_text":"local cost substantially. ost In Fig. 1(c), we depict another situation where the topology old heavi","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-08.png","occurrence_role":"example"},"source_text":"In this paper, we have addressed a problem in which several microgrids interact by exchanging energy in order to minimize the global operation cost while still satisfying their local demands."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g08-e008","location":{"location_id":"src-f5dd785b87-p02-c2r4__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":2,"printed_page":null,"section":"II. System Model","anchor_text":"ra- Finally, we further assume that all μGs agree to cooperate gly, with one another in order to min","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-02.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"Finally, we further assume that all μGs agree to cooperate with one another in order to minimize the total cost of the system. In other words, the energy quantities exchanged by the interconnected μGs form the equilibrium point of the following minimization problem:"},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g15-e015","location":{"location_id":"src-f5dd785b87-p03-c2r2__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":3,"printed_page":null,"section":"III-A. Decentralizing the Problem","anchor_text":"i i i i i at {ε(s)i },{Ei,j} i=1 + eTi AT γ(E(b)i ) i=1 s.t. Ei,j ≥0 ∀i,j ks ey E(c)i + ε(s)i −eTi A","equation_id":"(4)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-03.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"C^* = min_{ {ε_i^{(s)}},{E_{i,j}} } ∑_{i=1}^M C_i(E_i^{(c)} + ε_i^{(s)} - e_i^T A^T E_i^{(b)}) + ∑_{i=1}^M e_i^T A^T γ(E_i^{(b)}) s.t. E_{i,j} ≥ 0 ∀i,j, E_i^{(c)} + ε_i^{(s)} - e_i^T A^T E_i^{(b)} ≥ 0 ∀i, ε_i^{(s)} = e_i^T A E_i^{(s)} ∀i. (4) The idea is that, for each μG, we first use the new variable ε_i^{(s)} to represent the energy sold by μG-i and only later force it to be equal to all the energy bought by other μGs from μG-i, i.e., ε_i^{(s)}=e_i^T A E_i^{(s)}, which is the coupling constraint."}]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R003-C004","kind":"mathematical","role":"core","statement":"Each microgrid's generation plus imports must equal consumption plus exports. Local subproblems constrain offered sales, purchases and residual generation to be nonnegative. Scope: Feasible inter-microgrid allocations and the local optimization subproblems used to compute them.","expression":"E_i^{(g)} + e_i^T A^T E_i^{(b)} = E_i^{(c)} + e_i^T A E_i^{(s)} (1); E_i^{(c)} + ε_i^{(s)} - e_i^T A^T E_i^{(b)} ≥ 0 ∀i; ε_i^{(s)} ≥ 0; E_{j,i} ≥ 0 ∀j","formalization_origin":"source","template_id":null,"members":["clearing.inter_area"],"variable_refs":["v141-c-R003-x-Eg","v141-c-R003-x-Eb","v141-c-R003-x-Es","v141-c-R003-x-eps","v141-c-R003-x-Eij"],"parameter_refs":["v141-c-R003-theta-Ec","v141-c-R003-theta-A","v141-c-R003-theta-ei"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g04-e004","v141-b-g16-e016","v141-b-g05-e005"],"ref":"27-multiple_microgrid_trading::v141-c-R003::v141-c-R003-C004","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R003","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g04-e004","location":{"location_id":"src-f5dd785b87-p02-c2r1__v141-a-M01-participation","page":2,"printed_page":null,"section":"II. System Model","anchor_text":"IEEE TRANSACTIONS ON INDUSTRIAL ELECTRONICS, VOL. 62, NO. 4, APRIL 2015 we from an economical point ","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-02.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"Consider a system composed of M interconnected microgrids (μGs) operating in islanded mode. During each scheduling interval, each microgrid μG-i generates E_i^{(g)} units of energy and consumes E_i^{(c)} units of energy. Moreover, μG-i may be allowed to sell energy E_{i,j} to μG-j, j≠i, and to buy energy E_{k,i} from μG-k, k≠i. Then, the energy equilibrium within the μG requires E_i^{(g)} + e_i^T A^T E_i^{(b)} = E_i^{(c)} + e_i^T A E_i^{(s)} (1)"},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g16-e016","location":{"location_id":"src-f5dd785b87-p03-c2r3__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":3,"printed_page":null,"section":"III-A. Decentralizing the Problem","anchor_text":"we q y, ( )], y g the M coupling constraints and solving the following dual he problem: er C∗= max C","equation_id":"(5)-(6)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-03.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"C^* = max_λ C(λ) (5) where C(λ) = ∑_{i=1}^M C_i^{(l)}(λ) with the terms C_i^{(l)}(λ) = min_{ε_i^{(s)},E_i^{(b)}} C_i(ε_i^{(s)},E_i^{(b)},λ) s.t. ε_i^{(s)} ≥ 0, E_{j,i} ≥ 0 ∀j, E_i^{(c)} + ε_i^{(s)} - e_i^T A^T E_i^{(b)} ≥ 0. (6)"},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g05-e005","location":{"location_id":"src-f5dd785b87-p02-c2r1__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":2,"printed_page":null,"section":"II. System Model","anchor_text":"IEEE TRANSACTIONS ON INDUSTRIAL ELECTRONICS, VOL. 62, NO. 4, APRIL 2015 we from an economical point ","equation_id":"(1)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-02.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"E_i^{(g)} + e_i^T A^T E_i^{(b)} = E_i^{(c)} + e_i^T A E_i^{(s)} (1)"}]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R003-C005","kind":"mathematical","role":"core","statement":"The aggregate energy sold by each microgrid must equal the total energy bought from it by the other microgrids; this coupling constraint governs feasible allocations rather than requiring agreement at every intermediate iteration. Scope: Feasible solutions of the reformulated inter-microgrid allocation problem (4).","expression":"ε_i^{(s)} = e_i^T A E_i^{(s)} ∀i","formalization_origin":"source","template_id":null,"members":["clearing.inter_area"],"variable_refs":["v141-c-R003-x-eps","v141-c-R003-x-Es","v141-c-R003-x-Eij"],"parameter_refs":["v141-c-R003-theta-A","v141-c-R003-theta-ei"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g15-e015","v141-b-g16-e016"],"ref":"27-multiple_microgrid_trading::v141-c-R003::v141-c-R003-C005","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R003","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g15-e015","location":{"location_id":"src-f5dd785b87-p03-c2r2__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":3,"printed_page":null,"section":"III-A. Decentralizing the Problem","anchor_text":"i i i i i at {ε(s)i },{Ei,j} i=1 + eTi AT γ(E(b)i ) i=1 s.t. Ei,j ≥0 ∀i,j ks ey E(c)i + ε(s)i −eTi A","equation_id":"(4)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-03.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"C^* = min_{ {ε_i^{(s)}},{E_{i,j}} } ∑_{i=1}^M C_i(E_i^{(c)} + ε_i^{(s)} - e_i^T A^T E_i^{(b)}) + ∑_{i=1}^M e_i^T A^T γ(E_i^{(b)}) s.t. E_{i,j} ≥ 0 ∀i,j, E_i^{(c)} + ε_i^{(s)} - e_i^T A^T E_i^{(b)} ≥ 0 ∀i, ε_i^{(s)} = e_i^T A E_i^{(s)} ∀i. (4) The idea is that, for each μG, we first use the new variable ε_i^{(s)} to represent the energy sold by μG-i and only later force it to be equal to all the energy bought by other μGs from μG-i, i.e., ε_i^{(s)}=e_i^T A E_i^{(s)}, which is the coupling constraint."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g16-e016","location":{"location_id":"src-f5dd785b87-p03-c2r3__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":3,"printed_page":null,"section":"III-A. Decentralizing the Problem","anchor_text":"we q y, ( )], y g the M coupling constraints and solving the following dual he problem: er C∗= max C","equation_id":"(5)-(6)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-03.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"C^* = max_λ C(λ) (5) where C(λ) = ∑_{i=1}^M C_i^{(l)}(λ) with the terms C_i^{(l)}(λ) = min_{ε_i^{(s)},E_i^{(b)}} C_i(ε_i^{(s)},E_i^{(b)},λ) s.t. ε_i^{(s)} ≥ 0, E_{j,i} ≥ 0 ∀j, E_i^{(c)} + ε_i^{(s)} - e_i^T A^T E_i^{(b)} ≥ 0. (6)"}]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R003-C006","kind":"mathematical","role":"core","statement":"The allocated energy transfer E_{i,j} must be zero. Scope: Directed inter-microgrid transfer allocations. Condition: There is no directed connection from μG-i to μG-j, so a_{i,j}=0.","expression":"a_{i,j}=0 ⇒ E_{i,j}=0 MWh ∀i,j=1,...,M","formalization_origin":"source","template_id":null,"members":["clearing.inter_area"],"variable_refs":["v141-c-R003-x-Eij"],"parameter_refs":["v141-c-R003-theta-A"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g06-e006"],"ref":"27-multiple_microgrid_trading::v141-c-R003::v141-c-R003-C006","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R003","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g06-e006","location":{"location_id":"src-f5dd785b87-p02-c2r2__v141-a-M01-participation","page":2,"printed_page":null,"section":"II. System Model","anchor_text":"to sell energy Ei,j to μG j, j̸ i, and to buy energy Ek,i fromrgy μG-k, k̸=i. Then, the energy equil","equation_id":"(2)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-02.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"where the two M-dimensional column vectors, i.e., E_i^{(b)} = [E_{1,i} ... E_{M,i}]^T, E_i^{(s)} = [E_{i,1} ... E_{i,M}]^T (2) gather all the energy quantities bought and sold, respectively, by μG-i. In addition, we have introduced adjacency matrix A = [a_{i,j}]_{M×M}, where element a_{i,j} is equal to 1 if there exists a connection from μG-i to μG-j and 0 otherwise. Note that, generally, A may be nonsymmetric, meaning that at least two μGs are allowed to exchange energy in one direction only. By convention, we fix a_{i,i}=0. Moreover, a_{i,j}=0 ⇒ E_{i,j}=0 MWh for all i,j=1,...,M."}]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R003-C007","kind":"mathematical","role":"core","statement":"All generation and transfer cost functions must be positive valued, monotonically increasing, convex and twice differentiable. Scope: Applicability of the proposed distributed inter-microgrid cost-minimization method.","expression":"Each C_i(·) and γ(·) are positive valued, monotonically increasing, convex, and twice differentiable.","formalization_origin":"source","template_id":null,"members":["clearing.inter_area"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":["v141-c-R003-theta-Ci","v141-c-R003-theta-gamma"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g11-e011"],"ref":"27-multiple_microgrid_trading::v141-c-R003::v141-c-R003-C007","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R003","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g11-e011","location":{"location_id":"src-f5dd785b87-p03-c1r2__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":3,"printed_page":null,"section":"II-A. Cost Functions","anchor_text":"where achieving the global objective implies a cost reduction at every μG. A. Cost Functions As afor","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-03.png","occurrence_role":"condition"},"source_text":"As aforementioned, the algorithm proposed hereafter works with any set of generation/transfer cost functions as long as they all satisfy some mild convexity constraints. Specifically, it is required that {C_i(·)} and γ(·) are positive valued, monotonically increasing, convex, and twice differentiable. Although these requirements may seem abstract and distant from real systems, one should take into account that the cost function of a common electrical generator (e.g., oil, coal, nuclear,...) is often modeled as a quadratic polynomial C(x)=a+bx+cx^2, where coefficients a, b, and c depend on the generator type (see [30] and particularly [31]). Such a generation model clearly satisfies our assumptions, and without available counterexamples, we extended it to the transfer cost function γ(·)."}]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R003-C008","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"Nominal generation and link-transfer bounds may be represented by steeply rising cost functions. A microgrid may exceed its nominal generation maximum at significant additional cost. Scope: Optional representation of nominal generation and transfer-capacity bounds in the inter-microgrid allocation problem. Condition: Nominal upper bounds are introduced through cost-function soft constraints.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["clearing.inter_area"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g12-e012"],"ref":"27-multiple_microgrid_trading::v141-c-R003::v141-c-R003-C008","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R003","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g12-e012","location":{"location_id":"src-f5dd785b87-p03-c1r3__v141-a-M01-participation","page":3,"printed_page":null,"section":"II-A. Cost Functions","anchor_text":"our assumptions, and without available counterexamples, we q extended it to the transfer cost functi","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-03.png","occurrence_role":"exception"},"source_text":"It is worth commenting here that the assumptions on the cost functions also allow for a simple way to introduce upper bounds on the energy generated by the μGs or supported by the transfer connections. Indeed, one can introduce soft constraints by designing the cost function with a steep rise at the nominal maximum value (see also the first paragraph in Section IV, where we comment on the cost functions used for simulation). By doing so, the maximum generated/transferred energy quantities are directly controlled by the cost functions without the need for hard constraints (i.e., well-specified inequalities such as E_i^{(g)} ≤ E_{i,max}^{(g)}) that would increase the complexity of the minimization problem in (3). Note that this expedient translates, somehow, to a more flexible system, i.e., when needed, a μG can produce more energy than the nominal maximum if it is willing to pay an (significant) extra cost. Indeed, this situation arises in practical systems when backup generators are activated."}]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R010-C004","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"The intermicrogrid market-clearing process adjusts energy prices according to the law of demand until global demand matches offer. Scope: The distributed market-clearing phase for exchanges among interconnected islanded microgrids. Condition: During iterative clearing, until global energy demand matches the energy offer.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["clearing.inter_area","pricing.inter_area"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g03-e10","v141-b-g24-e024"],"ref":"27-multiple_microgrid_trading::v141-c-R010::v141-c-R010-C004","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R010","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g03-e10","location":{"location_id":"src-f5dd785b87-p08-c2r4__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":8,"printed_page":null,"section":"V. Conclusion","anchor_text":"G 1 keeps local cost functions and local consumption private. More speciﬁcally, each algorithm itera","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-08.png","occurrence_role":"restatement"},"source_text":"Next, during the market clearing process, energy prices are adjusted according to the law of demand."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g24-e024","location":{"location_id":"src-f5dd785b87-p04-c1r4__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":4,"printed_page":null,"section":"III-A. Decentralizing the Problem","anchor_text":", g ining interpretation: Each Lagrange multiplier λi may be under- costood as the price per energy ","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"According to this view, updating step (8) is clearing the market, i.e., prices should be modified until, globally, the energy demand matches the energy offer. Note that (8) is an example of the law of demand: If the energy offered by μG-i ε_i^{(s)}[k] is less than all the energy demanded by the neighboring μGs from μG-i, i.e., e_i^T A E_i^{(s)}, then the selling price must increase, and λ_i[k+1] ≥ λ_i[k]."}]}],"variables":[{"variable_id":"v141-c-R003-x-Eij","symbol":"E_{i,j}","meaning":"Directed energy transfer allocated from microgrid i to microgrid j in the inter-microgrid clearing problem.","kind":"operational","domain":"E_{i,j} ≥ 0 for all i,j in problem (3)/(4); directed transfer quantity.","unit":"MWh","indices":["i=1,...,M","j=1,...,M"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g04-e004","v141-b-g06-e006","v141-b-g15-e015"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R003","declaration_ref":"27-multiple_microgrid_trading::v141-c-R003::x::v141-c-R003-x-Eij"},{"variable_id":"v141-c-R003-x-eps","symbol":"ε_i^{(s)}","meaning":"Energy sold by microgrid i in the reformulated inter-microgrid allocation problem (4), later forced equal to the energy bought from i by other microgrids.","kind":"operational","domain":"Decision variable in (4); local subproblem (6) explicitly imposes ε_i^{(s)} ≥ 0.","unit":"MWh","indices":["i=1,...,M"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g15-e015","v141-b-g16-e016"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R003","declaration_ref":"27-multiple_microgrid_trading::v141-c-R003::x::v141-c-R003-x-eps"},{"variable_id":"v141-c-R003-x-Eg","symbol":"E_i^{(g)}","meaning":"Energy generated by microgrid i during the scheduling interval in the system model.","kind":"operational","domain":"Energy generation quantity in MWh as used in balance (1); the supplied evidence does not state an explicit sign bound on E_i^{(g)}.","unit":"MWh","indices":["i=1,...,M"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g04-e004"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R003","declaration_ref":"27-multiple_microgrid_trading::v141-c-R003::x::v141-c-R003-x-Eg"},{"variable_id":"v141-c-R003-x-Eb","symbol":"E_i^{(b)}","meaning":"M-dimensional vector collecting the energy quantities bought by microgrid i from the other microgrids.","kind":"operational","domain":"Derived vector E_i^{(b)} = [E_{1,i} ... E_{M,i}]^T with entries E_{j,i} from directed transfers.","unit":"MWh","indices":["i=1,...,M"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g06-e006"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R003","declaration_ref":"27-multiple_microgrid_trading::v141-c-R003::x::v141-c-R003-x-Eb"},{"variable_id":"v141-c-R003-x-Es","symbol":"E_i^{(s)}","meaning":"M-dimensional vector collecting the energy quantities sold by microgrid i to the other microgrids.","kind":"operational","domain":"Derived vector E_i^{(s)} = [E_{i,1} ... E_{i,M}]^T with entries E_{i,j} from directed transfers.","unit":"MWh","indices":["i=1,...,M"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g06-e006"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R003","declaration_ref":"27-multiple_microgrid_trading::v141-c-R003::x::v141-c-R003-x-Es"}],"parameters":[{"parameter_id":"v141-c-R003-theta-M","symbol":"M","meaning":"Number of interconnected microgrids participating in the clearing problem.","value":{"kind":"symbolic","number_value":null,"text_value":"M"},"unit":null,"indices":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g04-e004"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R003","declaration_ref":"27-multiple_microgrid_trading::v141-c-R003::theta::v141-c-R003-theta-M"},{"parameter_id":"v141-c-R003-theta-Ec","symbol":"E_i^{(c)}","meaning":"Local energy consumption or demand of microgrid i during the scheduling interval.","value":{"kind":"symbolic","number_value":null,"text_value":"E_i^{(c)}"},"unit":"MWh","indices":["i=1,...,M"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g04-e004","v141-b-g39-e039"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R003","declaration_ref":"27-multiple_microgrid_trading::v141-c-R003::theta::v141-c-R003-theta-Ec"},{"parameter_id":"v141-c-R003-theta-A","symbol":"A","meaning":"Adjacency matrix A = [a_{i,j}] of the directed microgrid connection topology, with a_{i,j}=1 if a connection from microgrid i to j exists and 0 otherwise; a_{i,i}=0 by convention.","value":{"kind":"symbolic","number_value":null,"text_value":"A = [a_{i,j}]_{M×M}"},"unit":null,"indices":["i=1,...,M","j=1,...,M"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g06-e006"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R003","declaration_ref":"27-multiple_microgrid_trading::v141-c-R003::theta::v141-c-R003-theta-A"},{"parameter_id":"v141-c-R003-theta-ei","symbol":"e_i","meaning":"The i-th column of the M×M identity matrix, used to select the i-th component of vector expressions.","value":{"kind":"symbolic","number_value":null,"text_value":"e_i"},"unit":null,"indices":["i=1,...,M"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g10-e010"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R003","declaration_ref":"27-multiple_microgrid_trading::v141-c-R003::theta::v141-c-R003-theta-ei"},{"parameter_id":"v141-c-R003-theta-Ci","symbol":"C_i(·)","meaning":"Generation cost function of microgrid i.","value":{"kind":"symbolic","number_value":null,"text_value":"C_i(·)"},"unit":null,"indices":["i=1,...,M"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g11-e011"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R003","declaration_ref":"27-multiple_microgrid_trading::v141-c-R003::theta::v141-c-R003-theta-Ci"},{"parameter_id":"v141-c-R003-theta-gamma","symbol":"γ(·)","meaning":"Transfer cost function common to all directed links.","value":{"kind":"symbolic","number_value":null,"text_value":"γ(·)"},"unit":null,"indices":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g11-e011"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R003","declaration_ref":"27-multiple_microgrid_trading::v141-c-R003::theta::v141-c-R003-theta-gamma"}]}],"source_definitions":[{"unit_id":"04-energy_collectives::v141-a-M02::clearing.inter_area","stage":"L5","subtype":"geography_weighted_flow_allocation","projection_status":"clear","target_definition":"The inter-regional configuration includes geographical transaction costs in g=γ_imp q_imp+γ_exp q_exp+γ_geoᵀ|v|, and satisfies Σ_{j∈N1}q_j=v_12+v_13 and Σ_{j∈N2}q_j=−v_12+v_23.","form_kind":"mathematical","identity_fields":["Object: energy flows between neighborhoods and neighborhood net exchange.","Core operation: include geographical cost by the absolute value of flows and impose directed-flow balance.","Output: inter-regional configuration under geographical cost and neighborhood balance constraints."],"variation_fields":["Three neighborhoods N1, N2, N3.","Flow vector v=(v_12,v_13,v_23).","Geographical cost vector γ_geo and import and export coefficients.","Applies to cases that adopt the geographical preference model."],"constraint_refs":["04-energy_collectives::v141-c-R016::v141-c-R016-C001","04-energy_collectives::v141-c-R016::v141-c-R016-C002"],"paper_id":"04-energy_collectives","mechanism_id":"v141-a-M02","function":"clearing.inter_area","template_code":"JOC"},{"unit_id":"27-multiple_microgrid_trading::v141-a-M01::clearing.inter_area","stage":"L5","subtype":"joint_cost_minimizing_allocation","projection_status":"clear","target_definition":"Jointly determine ε_i^{(s)} and E_{i,j}, minimizing ∑_{i=1}^M C_i(E_i^{(c)}+ε_i^{(s)}−e_i^T A^T E_i^{(b)})+∑_{i=1}^M e_i^T A^T γ(E_i^{(b)}), subject to local energy balance, nonnegative quantities, supply-demand coupling, and zero-transaction conditions for no connection.","form_kind":"mathematical","identity_fields":["Object: mutually coupled generation and cross-entity energy trading configuration.","Core operation: minimize total generation and transmission costs within a common feasible region.","Output: joint configuration satisfying demand and transaction consistency."],"variation_fields":["All microgrids agree to cooperate to minimize total system cost.","The directed adjacency matrix A may be asymmetric.","A feasible solution requires ε_i^{(s)}=e_i^T A E_i^{(s)}, and it is not required that each round's local solution already satisfy it.","C_i and γ must be positive, monotonically increasing, convex, and twice differentiable.","Nominal generation and transmission upper limits can be represented as soft constraints by steeply increasing costs and cannot be rewritten as hard upper limits.","Achieve supply-demand consistency through local solving and iterative price adjustment."],"constraint_refs":["27-multiple_microgrid_trading::v141-c-R003::v141-c-R003-C003","27-multiple_microgrid_trading::v141-c-R003::v141-c-R003-C004","27-multiple_microgrid_trading::v141-c-R003::v141-c-R003-C005","27-multiple_microgrid_trading::v141-c-R003::v141-c-R003-C006","27-multiple_microgrid_trading::v141-c-R003::v141-c-R003-C007","27-multiple_microgrid_trading::v141-c-R003::v141-c-R003-C008","27-multiple_microgrid_trading::v141-c-R010::v141-c-R010-C004"],"paper_id":"27-multiple_microgrid_trading","mechanism_id":"v141-a-M01","function":"clearing.inter_area","template_code":"JOC"}],"source_variants":[{"unit_id":"04-energy_collectives::v141-a-M02::clearing.inter_area","source_definition":"The inter-regional configuration includes geographical transaction costs in g=γ_imp q_imp+γ_exp q_exp+γ_geoᵀ|v|, and satisfies Σ_{j∈N1}q_j=v_12+v_13 and Σ_{j∈N2}q_j=−v_12+v_23.","retained_variants":["Applies only to the three neighborhoods that adopt the geographical preference model; v=(v_12,v_13,v_23), and each interconnection retains its own geographical weight.","g retains the original signs of the import and export terms and the absolute flow charges, and is not rewritten as the differentiable transmission cost function of 27.","This projection uses only the explicitly listed N1 and N2 balances; the complete quotation's N3 equation repeats v_13, which is not corrected without authorization or used to prove whole-network conservation.","The flow domain, units, and capacity limits not explicitly given by this member remain unknown."],"constraint_refs":["04-energy_collectives::v141-c-R016::v141-c-R016-C001","04-energy_collectives::v141-c-R016::v141-c-R016-C002"]},{"unit_id":"27-multiple_microgrid_trading::v141-a-M01::clearing.inter_area","source_definition":"Jointly determine ε_i^{(s)} and E_{i,j}, minimizing ∑_{i=1}^M C_i(E_i^{(c)}+ε_i^{(s)}−e_i^T A^T E_i^{(b)})+∑_{i=1}^M e_i^T A^T γ(E_i^{(b)}), subject to local energy balance, nonnegative quantities, supply-demand coupling, and zero-transaction conditions for no connection.","retained_variants":["Islanded interconnected microgrids must agree to cooperate to minimize total generation and transmission costs; their objective is not reduced to geographical charges.","E_{i,j} is measured in MWh and is nonnegative; A may be asymmetric, and when a_{i,j}=0 the transaction is zero; local sold quantity, purchased quantity, and residual generation retain nonnegativity conditions.","ε_i^{(s)}=e_i^T A E_i^{(s)} constrains the final feasible configuration; it is not required that each round's local solution already be consistent.","C_i and γ must be positive, monotonically increasing, convex, and twice differentiable; these requirements are not imposed on 04.","Nominal generation and transmission upper limits can be represented as soft constraints by steeply increasing costs, allowing the nominal values to be exceeded after paying additional costs.","Locally calculate purchase and sale quantities and exchange quotations, then adjust prices until supply and demand are consistent; this process is an implementation of the configuration, not a price loop shared by the two members."],"constraint_refs":["27-multiple_microgrid_trading::v141-c-R003::v141-c-R003-C001","27-multiple_microgrid_trading::v141-c-R003::v141-c-R003-C002","27-multiple_microgrid_trading::v141-c-R003::v141-c-R003-C003","27-multiple_microgrid_trading::v141-c-R003::v141-c-R003-C004","27-multiple_microgrid_trading::v141-c-R003::v141-c-R003-C005","27-multiple_microgrid_trading::v141-c-R003::v141-c-R003-C006","27-multiple_microgrid_trading::v141-c-R003::v141-c-R003-C007","27-multiple_microgrid_trading::v141-c-R003::v141-c-R003-C008","27-multiple_microgrid_trading::v141-c-R010::v141-c-R010-C004"]}],"source_parent_definition":{"definition":"Through a source-explicit regional balance relation, associate regional net exchange with cross-regional directed flows, and select the cross-regional configuration under an objective that includes interconnection flow transaction costs.","object":"Inter-regional interconnection flows and their coupled regional net exchange and generation-consumption configuration.","inputs":"Regional supply-demand or net-exchange information, interconnection representation, and flow cost rules; external exchange charges, generation costs, and interconnection permissions are provided according to the source.","operation":"Aggregate the associated regional net exchange using signed interconnection flows, include each interconnection's flow cost in configuration selection, and satisfy the quantity and coupling conditions specified by the source.","outputs":"Cross-regional flows satisfying the specified regional balance, and the related net exchange and production configuration.","boundary":"Limited to regional flow configuration in which L5/JOC costs participate; an overall cost objective or internal zero-sum alone is insufficient for inclusion. Retain the original balance signs, cost functions, and quantity domains; do not complete unsupported balance equations; do not change soft capacity into a hard upper limit."},"functional":{"is_child":false,"subdivision_pending":false},"canonical_pointer":"/nodes/248"});
